# Content is byte-stable across platforms.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows checks files out with CRLF while git stores LF.
# For source and config that is harmless. For content it is not, for two reasons:
#
#   1. `check:content` compares the committed artifact against a fresh generation
#      byte for byte. A CRLF checkout makes that comparison fail on every fresh
#      Windows clone, which turns a real drift gate into noise.
#   2. The generator embeds the markdown source inside the artifact and then into
#      D1. A CRLF checkout would put CRLF into the stored post body and into the
#      markdown served by /blog/:slug.md, so the same commit would produce
#      different content depending on which machine ran the generator.
#
# Pinning these paths to LF makes the generator's output depend on the content
# alone. Scoped deliberately: the rest of the tree keeps the default behaviour.

content/posts/*.md      text eol=lf
content/generated/*.json text eol=lf
